3194139534576 17592186045417 true] [17592186426285 1072 #uuid "abe1f7cc-b85d-54fe-b1a8-40e514a9b913" 13194139534576 17592186045417 true] [17592186426285 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 7 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534576 17592186045417 true] [17592186426286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426284 13194139534576 17592186045417 true] [17592186426286 1069 "q-81851" 13194139534576 17592186045417 true] [17592186426286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534576 17592186045417 true] [17592186426286 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}a & 3 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & b \\\\ 7 & c & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534576 17592186045417 true] [17592186426286 1072 #uuid "b83d51a4-64e8-5d15-b4eb-1a14ddbe497b" 13194139534576 17592186045417 true] [17592186426286 1074 "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1},{e}_{2},$ and ${e}_{3},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors in $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{3}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5.$" 13194139534576 17592186045417 true] [17592186426287 1058 #edb/ref 17592186426289 13194139534576 17592186045417 true] [17592186426287 1062 #uuid "047626a4-44a1-518d-a9b8-705468162bf9" 13194139534576 17592186045417 true] [17592186426287 1063 #edb/ref 17592186394305 13194139534576 17592186045417 true] [17592186426288 1058 #edb/ref 17592186426287 13194139534576 17592186045417 true] [17592186426288 1062 #uuid "14b98c73-3778-5d7d-bd8f-d1f3c481dbdf" 13194139534576 17592186045417 true] [17592186426288 1063 #edb/ref 17592186426286 13194139534576 17592186045417 true] [17592186426289 1058 #edb/ref 17592186426291 13194139534576 17592186045417 true] [17592186426289 1062 #uuid "2efe5e7e-1009-5580-b1b3-51c076c0ff38" 13194139534576 1